      "title": "Brisbane and Sydney hit hardest by falling house prices as 5.2% shaved from Australian property values",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/01/brisbane-sydney-hit-hardest-by-falling-house-prices",
      "description": "<p>Cotality data shows median house prices fell in September as interest rates hit borrowing capacity</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Housing prices are falling in almost every suburb in Australia’s biggest cities, with values dropping faster in Brisbane than Sydney as the slowdown intensifies.</p><p>Prices nationally have fallen 5.2% from their peak in March to where they were a year ago, Cotality data released on Thursday shows.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/01/brisbane-sydney-hit-hardest-by-falling-house-prices\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-30T15:00:51"
    },
    {
      "title": "Australian colleges warned ‘do not try to game’ student visas after last-minute enrolments for cancelled diploma soar",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/01/australia-private-colleges-student-visa-enrolments-soar-for-cancelled-diploma-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Julian Hill says educational providers are being closely watched by government after course attracted non-genuine students</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">new political email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Private colleges have issued nearly 3,000 last-minute enrolments to a diploma course that is being cancelled after allegedly being “misused” by non-genuine students, as Labor threatens to suspend providers that are manipulating new rules.</p><p>Guardian Australia revealed last month that <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/27/australia-private-colleges-discount-deregistered-management-diploma-visa-crackdown-immigration-ntwnfb\">private colleges were offering discounts to rush students</a> into the graduate diploma of management (learning) ahead of its deregistration on 5 October, which the government says was being used by some non-genuine students to remain in the country. The assistant minister for international education, Julian Hill, said at the time the system had been “misused”.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/01/australia-private-colleges-student-visa-enrolments-soar-for-cancelled-diploma-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-30T15:00:45"
    },
    {
      "title": "David Shoebridge wants the Greens to ‘seize this moment’. Is his more combative style the answer?",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/30/australian-greens-david-shoebridge-new-leader-style-analysis",
      "description": "<p>New leader says his party must connect with millions of disaffected voters and ‘make them believe in politics again’</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/sep/30/labor-anthony-albanese-coalition-polling-new-greens-leader-openai-hack-ntwnfb\">Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates</a></p></li><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">new political email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>As One Nation’s polling surged this year, and Pauline Hanson ate away at the votes of first the Coalition and then Labor, one curious mainstay emerged; the Greens remained seemingly anchored to the 10-12% range, <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/29/guardian-essential-poll-coalitions-primary-vote-sinks-to-lowest-ever-as-majority-support-hardline-immigration-ideas\">no matter which poll you look at</a>.</p><p>That’s despite the fact that, arguably, there’s rarely been more fertile political ground for the Greens leader to reap rewards; a centrist Labor government with a big majority but increasingly on the nose, a resurgent anti-immigration populist party backed by Australia’s richest mining billionaire, a Coalition in disarray, a cost-of-living crisis set ablaze by rising interest rates and inflation, and a public increasingly alarmed about the nexus of big tech, big datacentres and shadowy AI oligarchs operating with impunity.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/30/australian-greens-david-shoebridge-new-leader-style-analysis\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-30T07:12:22"
    }
  ]
